---
title: "Example: who reaches the jobs in São Paulo, Rio de Janeiro and Belo Horizonte"
description: >
  Access to jobs, schools and health facilities by public transport in three
  Brazilian capitals with an official, open GTFS feed, by income, on the grid
  of Ipea's Access to Opportunities Project; and, in Porto Alegre, what a
  fare budget leaves of that access.
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
# This article is precomputed: its source is capitals.Rmd.orig, knitted into
# capitals.Rmd by `bash site/figures/make.sh --capitals` (the data takes 1 GB
# of downloads and osmium, and the routing half an hour to an hour: too much for every
# site build). Only the chunks written out below by cat() run when pkgdown
# renders it.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
# run in a directory where capitals-data is the cache of site/figures/capitals-data.R
work <- file.path(tempdir(), "capitals")
dir.create(work, showWarnings = FALSE)
if (nzchar(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_CAPITALS_CACHE")) && !file.exists(file.path(work, "capitals-data"))) {
  file.symlink(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_CAPITALS_CACHE"), file.path(work, "capitals-data"))
}
knitr::opts_knit$set(root.dir = work)
# while the text is being written: keep the results of the slow chunks
if (nzchar(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_KNIT_CACHE"))) {
  knitr::opts_chunk$set(cache.path = paste0(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_KNIT_CACHE"), "/"))
}
slow <- nzchar(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_KNIT_CACHE"))
options(width = 100)
live <- function(code, opts = "echo = FALSE") {
  cat("```{r, ", opts, "}\n", code, "\n```\n", sep = "")
}
pct <- function(x, digits = 0) paste0(formatC(100 * x, format = "f", digits = digits), "%")
num <- function(x) format(round(x), big.mark = ",")
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('source("setup.R")', "include = FALSE")
```

How many jobs can a resident reach in an hour by bus and train, and does
the answer depend on how much the household earns? This example measures it
for three Brazilian capitals, from their official GTFS feeds as published
in November 2026, and then, in a fourth, asks what is left of that access
when the fare is limited.

Everything is computed by peabiru; the output shown is what the code prints,
computed on 2026-11-12. The [Python example](../../python/example-capitals/)
repeats Belo Horizonte and the fares, and prints the same numbers.

## Which cities

Five capitals were considered: São Paulo, Rio de Janeiro, Belo Horizonte,
Fortaleza and Curitiba. Three are used:

| City | Feed | What it has | Version, validity | Licence |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| São Paulo | [SPTrans](https://www.sptrans.com.br/desenvolvedores/), as the [Mobility Database](https://mobilitydatabase.org/feeds/gtfs/mdb-8) fetched it from SPTrans on 2026-11-12 | 1,350 bus routes, 9 of the Metrô and 7 of CPTM, all by frequency (`frequencies.txt`) | `agency_url` carries `versao=111126`; calendar 2023-10-01 to 2027-06-01 | public data: SPTrans publishes the file for registered developers; neither its page nor the Mobility Database names a licence |
| Rio de Janeiro | [Secretaria Municipal de Transportes](https://www.data.rio/search?q=gtfs) (SMTR), "GTFS do Rio de Janeiro" on data.rio, file at `dados.mobilidade.rio/gtfs/schedule` | municipal buses and BRT, nearly all by frequency (17,275 of 17,361 trips); **no** metro, suburban trains (SuperVia), light rail (VLT) or ferries | file of 2026-06-10; `feed_info` 2024-01-01 to 2026-12-31 | CC BY 4.0 |
| Belo Horizonte | [Superintendência de Mobilidade](https://dados.pbh.gov.br/dataset/gtfs) (SUMOB), "GTFS" on dados.pbh.gov.br | the conventional and the supplementary bus systems, by timetable; **no** metro | version 202611111921 (rewritten every day at the same address); `feed_info` 2023-09-07 to 2026-12-31 | CC BY |

Two were left out:

- **Fortaleza.** The city publishes Etufor's feed on
  [dados.fortaleza.ce.gov.br](https://dados.fortaleza.ce.gov.br/dataset/especificacao-geral-feed-transito-gtfs-010-2025)
  (licence "Other (Open)"), but the file there on 2026-11-12 was of
  2026-04-01 and its calendar runs from 2026-04-03 to 2026-07-03: it
  describes no day after that, and the analysis here is of November 2026.
- **Curitiba.** No download published by URBS was found: the only feed in
  the Mobility Database's catalogue is kept by a third party on GitHub, and
  the city's open-data portal refused our requests (HTTP 403) on
  2026-11-12, so this was not taken further.

São Paulo's feed is the official one and is public data: SPTrans
publishes it for registered developers, without naming a licence. It is
used here as the Mobility Database redistributes it, and nothing of it is
copied into this repository.

## The data

| Data | Source | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| Streets | [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright), extract of the Sudeste region by [Geofabrik](https://download.geofabrik.de/south-america/brazil/sudeste.html), dated 2026-11-01 (863 MB; MD5 `15c2834145c0273c96b5e7dee21c1109`, as Geofabrik publishes) | ODbL 1.0 |
| GTFS | the three feeds above (14, 25 and 52 MB) | above |
| Municipal boundaries | [IBGE, malhas municipais](https://servicodados.ibge.gov.br/api/docs/malhas?versao=3) (API, version 3) | public data, IBGE |
| Cells, population, income | Ipea, [Access to Opportunities Project](https://www.ipea.gov.br/acessooportunidades/en/), data release v1.0.0 of [aopdata](https://github.com/ipeaGIT/aopdata): H3 cells of resolution 9 (about 350 m between centres) with the residents and the household income per person of the **Censo 2010** | the aopdata repository is MIT; no separate licence for the data is stated. Ipea asks for the citations at the end of this page |
| Jobs, schools, health facilities | the same release, land use of **2019**: formal jobs (RAIS), public schools (Censo Escolar), public health facilities (CNES), per cell | same |

Income is that of 2010 because nothing newer exists at this resolution: the
Censo 2022 tables by census tract on
[IBGE's server](https://ftp.ibge.gov.br/Censos/Censo_Demografico_2022/Agregados_por_Setores_Censitarios/Agregados_por_Setor_csv/)
(listing of 2026-11-12) have population, households, literacy, colour or
race and deaths, and none with income. The residents are those of 2010 too,
and the jobs those of 2019: the public transport is of November 2026, the
people and the opportunities are older.

The files are downloaded once into a directory of their own (set
`PEABIRU_EXAMPLE_DATA` to keep it somewhere else). Cutting the extract needs
[osmium](https://osmcode.org/osmium-tool/).

```{r download, message = FALSE}
library(peabiru)
library(sf)

data_dir <- Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_EXAMPLE_DATA", "capitals-data")
raw <- file.path(data_dir, "raw")
dir.create(raw, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
options(timeout = 3600, HTTPUserAgent = "peabiru-docs-examples")

fetch <- function(url, name = basename(url)) {
  dest <- file.path(raw, name)
  if (!file.exists(dest)) download.file(url, dest, mode = "wb", quiet = TRUE)
  dest
}
sudeste <- fetch("https://download.geofabrik.de/south-america/brazil/sudeste-261101.osm.pbf")
aop <- function(file) {
  fetch(paste0("https://github.com/ipeaGIT/aopdata/releases/download/v1.0.0/", file))
}

cities <- list(
  spo = list(name = "São Paulo", code = "3550308", feed = "sptrans.zip", url = paste0(
    "https://files.mobilitydatabase.org/mdb-8/mdb-8-202611120148/mdb-8-202611120148.zip")),
  rio = list(name = "Rio de Janeiro", code = "3304557", feed = "smtr.zip",
             url = "https://dados.mobilidade.rio/gtfs/schedule"),
  bho = list(name = "Belo Horizonte", code = "3106200", feed = "bhtrans.zip",
             url = "https://s3.amazonaws.com/mobilibus-uploads/gtfs/GTFSBHTRANS.zip")
)
feeds <- sapply(cities, function(city) fetch(city$url, city$feed))
data.frame(MB = round(file.size(feeds) / 1e6, 1),
           sha256 = substr(tools::sha256sum(feeds), 1, 16), row.names = basename(feeds))
```

Rio de Janeiro's and Belo Horizonte's addresses always serve the current
file, so a later download is another version of the network and gives other
numbers; the checksums above (their first 16 digits) identify the files
used here. The Mobility Database keeps São Paulo's under a dated address.

## Networks

The street network of each city is cut around the municipality and around
the stops of its feed (the trains of São Paulo's feed run to Jundiaí and
Mogi das Cruzes), with 3 km to spare. `build_network()` reads the directory
with the cut and the feed.

```{r networks}
utm <- 31983   # SIRGAS 2000 / UTM zone 23S, in metres, for the three cities
threads <- 4   # of the 10 of the laptop; the default is all of them

boundary_of <- function(code) {
  url <- paste0("https://servicodados.ibge.gov.br/api/v3/malhas/municipios/", code,
                "?formato=application/vnd.geo+json&qualidade=maxima")
  st_transform(st_make_valid(st_read(fetch(url, paste0(code, ".geojson")), quiet = TRUE)), utm)
}
stops_of <- function(zip) {
  s <- read.csv(unz(zip, "stops.txt"), colClasses = "character")
  s <- s[s$stop_lat != "" & s$stop_lon != "", ]
  st_transform(st_as_sf(data.frame(lon = as.numeric(s$stop_lon), lat = as.numeric(s$stop_lat)),
                        coords = c("lon", "lat"), crs = 4326), utm)
}

networks <- list()
for (k in names(cities)) {
  dir <- file.path(data_dir, k, "network")
  dir.create(dir, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
  file.copy(feeds[[k]], dir, overwrite = TRUE)
  hull <- st_convex_hull(st_union(stops_of(feeds[[k]])))
  area <- st_buffer(st_union(st_geometry(boundary_of(cities[[k]]$code)), hull), 3000)
  polygon <- file.path(data_dir, k, "cut.geojson")
  st_write(st_sf(geometry = st_transform(area, 4326)), polygon, delete_dsn = TRUE, quiet = TRUE)
  pbf <- file.path(dir, paste0(k, ".osm.pbf"))
  stopifnot(system2("osmium", c("extract", "--overwrite", "-p", polygon, sudeste, "-o", pbf)) == 0)
  networks[[k]] <- build_network(dir, n_threads = threads)
  cat(sprintf("%s: %.0f km2, %.1f MB\n", k, as.numeric(st_area(area)) / 1e6, file.size(pbf) / 1e6))
}
networks$spo
networks$rio
networks$bho
```

Before routing, two checks that cost nothing. `build_network()` refuses a
feed with errors of high priority, and `get_gtfs_errors()`
lists the others; `check_transit_availability()` says how much of each feed
runs on the day chosen, Tuesday 17 November 2026 (the Friday of that week
is a holiday).

```{r checks}
day <- as.Date("2026-11-17")
do.call(rbind, lapply(names(cities), function(k) {
  cbind(city = k, gtfs_errors = nrow(get_gtfs_errors(networks[[k]])),
        check_transit_availability(networks[[k]], dates = day))
}))
```

None of the three feeds has an error. Few of Belo Horizonte's services run
because its calendar has one service per kind of day and of system, and
only the weekday ones are due on a Tuesday.

## Residents, income and opportunities

Ipea's grid has, for each cell, the residents (`P001`), the average
household income per person (`R001`), the income decile of the cell among
the residents of the city (`R003`, 1 the poorest tenth), and the formal jobs
(`T001`), public schools (`E001`) and public health facilities (`S001`).
Each cell is routed from, and to, its centre.

```{r cells}
cells_of <- function(k) {
  grid <- st_read(aop(sprintf("hex_grid_%s.gpkg", k)), quiet = TRUE)[, "id_hex"]
  people <- read.csv(aop(sprintf("population_2010_%s.csv", k)))
  land <- read.csv(aop(sprintf("landuse_2019_%s.csv", k)))
  cells <- merge(grid, people[, c("id_hex", "P001", "R001", "R003")], by = "id_hex")
  cells <- merge(cells, land[, c("id_hex", "T001", "E001", "S001")], by = "id_hex")
  names(cells)[1:7] <- c("id", "population", "income", "decile", "jobs", "schools", "health")
  centre <- st_coordinates(st_transform(st_centroid(st_transform(st_geometry(cells), utm)), 4326))
  cells$lon <- round(centre[, 1], 6)
  cells$lat <- round(centre[, 2], 6)
  cells
}
grids <- lapply(setNames(names(cities), names(cities)), cells_of)
t(sapply(grids, function(g) c(cells = nrow(g), inhabited = sum(g$population > 0),
                              residents = sum(g$population), jobs = sum(g$jobs),
                              schools = sum(g$schools), health = sum(g$health))))
```

## Access at the morning peak

For every inhabited cell, the jobs, schools and health facilities of the
municipality reached within 30 and within 60 minutes, door to door, walking
and riding (`mode = c("WALK", "TRANSIT")`; walking at 3.6 km/h, the
default), leaving at each minute from 07:00 to 07:59; the travel time to
each destination is the median of those 60 departures (`percentiles = 50`).
São Paulo's feed and nearly all of Rio de Janeiro's give headways instead
of timetables; for those lines peabiru draws the departures at
random (Monte Carlo), and `seed` makes the draws, and so the result,
repeatable.

```{r access, cache = slow}
departure <- as.POSIXct("2026-11-17 07:00:00", tz = "America/Sao_Paulo")

access_of <- function(k) {
  cells <- st_drop_geometry(grids[[k]])
  origins <- cells[cells$population > 0, ]
  destinations <- cells[cells$jobs + cells$schools + cells$health > 0, ]
  a <- accessibility(
    networks[[k]], origins, destinations,
    opportunities_colnames = c("jobs", "schools", "health"),
    mode = c("WALK", "TRANSIT"), departure_datetime = departure, time_window = 60,
    percentiles = 50, cutoffs = c(30, 60), max_trip_duration = 60, seed = 42,
    n_threads = threads, progress = FALSE)
  cat(sprintf("%s: %d origins x %d destinations\n", k, nrow(origins), nrow(destinations)))
  # one row per cell, one column per opportunity and cutoff: jobs_60, ...
  a$what <- paste(a$opportunity, a$cutoff, sep = "_")
  wide <- reshape(as.data.frame(a[, c("id", "what", "accessibility")]), idvar = "id",
                  timevar = "what", direction = "wide")
  names(wide) <- sub("accessibility.", "", names(wide), fixed = TRUE)
  merge(origins, wide, by = "id")
}
access <- lapply(setNames(names(cities), names(cities)), access_of)
```

On the laptop this was written on (Apple M2 Pro, 4 of its 10 threads, busy
with other work at the time) the three calls took about 50 minutes
together, nearly all of it in São Paulo and Rio de Janeiro, and the R
session, with the three networks loaded, peaked at 3.8 GiB of memory.

As shares of the jobs of the municipality, weighted by residents:

```{r shares}
weighted_median <- function(x, w) {
  o <- order(x)
  x[o][which(cumsum(w[o]) >= sum(w) / 2)[1]]
}
shares <- t(sapply(names(cities), function(k) {
  a <- access[[k]]
  share <- a$jobs_60 / sum(grids[[k]]$jobs)
  c(mean = weighted.mean(share, a$population), median = weighted_median(share, a$population),
    none = weighted.mean(a$jobs_60 == 0, a$population))
}))
round(100 * shares, 1)
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
sh <- function(k, what) pct(shares[k, what], 1)
```

The average resident of São Paulo reaches `r sh("spo", "mean")` of the
city's formal jobs within an hour, and half the residents reach
`r sh("spo", "median")` or less; in Rio de Janeiro, `r sh("rio", "mean")`
and `r sh("rio", "median")`; in Belo Horizonte, a smaller and more compact
city, `r sh("bho", "mean")` and `r sh("bho", "median")`. The last column is
the share of residents who reach no job at all in an hour.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("jobs-60min-spo.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "A map of the municipality of São Paulo with cells coloured by the share of the formal jobs of the city reached within 60 minutes by public transport at the morning peak: highest in the centre and along the metro and train lines, lowest in the far south, east and north."')
live('fig("jobs-60min-rio.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "A map of the municipality of Rio de Janeiro with cells coloured by the share of the formal jobs of the city reached within 60 minutes by bus and BRT at the morning peak: highest in the centre and the north zone next to it, lowest in the west zone."')
live('fig("jobs-60min-bho.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "A map of the municipality of Belo Horizonte with cells coloured by the share of the formal jobs of the city reached within 60 minutes by bus at the morning peak: highest in and around the centre, lowest at the edges of the city."')
```

**What these numbers are not.** They count only the opportunities inside
the municipality, and only what the feed has: Rio de Janeiro without its
metro, trains, light rail and ferries, Belo Horizonte without its metro
line, São Paulo without the intermunicipal buses of EMTU. Rio's figures in
particular understate what its residents reach with the whole system, and
by how much is not measured here. Timetables and headways are what the
agencies planned, not what ran. Formal jobs leave out informal work.

## By income

The cells of each income decile, weighted by their residents: the mean
share of the city's jobs within an hour, and of its public schools and
health facilities within half an hour. The last row is the **Palma ratio**
of access: the mean of the richest tenth over the mean of the poorest four
tenths.

```{r income}
by_decile <- function(k, column) {
  a <- access[[k]][!is.na(access[[k]]$decile), ]
  share <- a[[column]] / sum(grids[[k]][[sub("_.*", "", column)]])
  means <- sapply(1:10, function(d) weighted.mean(share[a$decile == d], a$population[a$decile == d]))
  palma <- weighted.mean(share[a$decile == 10], a$population[a$decile == 10]) /
    weighted.mean(share[a$decile <= 4], a$population[a$decile <= 4])
  c(setNames(round(100 * means, 1), 1:10), palma = round(palma, 2))
}
sapply(names(cities), by_decile, column = "jobs_60")
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
palma <- sapply(names(cities), function(k) by_decile(k, "jobs_60")[["palma"]])
dec <- function(k, d) pct(by_decile(k, "jobs_60")[[d]] / 100, 1)
```

In all three cities access to jobs rises with income, decile after decile.
In São Paulo the richest tenth reaches `r dec("spo", 10)` of the jobs and
the poorest `r dec("spo", 1)`; the Palma ratio is
`r sprintf("%.1f", palma[["spo"]])`. In Rio de Janeiro, with buses and BRT
only, it is `r sprintf("%.1f", palma[["rio"]])`, and in Belo Horizonte
`r sprintf("%.1f", palma[["bho"]])`.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("access-income-capitals.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Three panels of box plots, one per city, of the share of the formal jobs of the city reached within 60 minutes by public transport from the cells of each income decile: in São Paulo, Rio de Janeiro and Belo Horizonte the boxes rise from the poorest decile to the richest."')
```

Schools and health facilities are spread over the city in a way jobs are
not, and half an hour is a more sensible limit for them:

```{r income-services}
sapply(names(cities), by_decile, column = "schools_30")
sapply(names(cities), by_decile, column = "health_30")
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("access-compare-capitals.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Three panels, for jobs within 60 minutes and public schools and public health facilities within 30 minutes, each with one line per city of the mean share of the opportunities of the city reached by public transport, by income decile."')
```

## Against Ipea's own estimates for 2019

The Access to Opportunities Project published, for the same cells and the
same jobs, the access by public transport at the morning peak of 2019,
computed with the r5r package from the feeds of that year. This is not a
check of peabiru (for that, see the [benchmarks](benchmarks.html), where
the two engines give identical matrices from identical inputs): the feeds
are seven
years apart, the street data differs, their window is 06:00 to 08:00, and
for Rio de Janeiro their network had the rail systems this one lacks. It
shows how far the picture of 2019 still holds.

```{r aop}
compare <- sapply(names(cities), function(k) {
  ipea <- read.csv(aop(sprintf("access_2019_public_transport_%s.csv", k)))
  ipea <- ipea[ipea$peak == 1, c("id_hex", "CMATT60")]
  both <- merge(access[[k]], ipea, by.x = "id", by.y = "id_hex")
  c(cells = nrow(both),
    peabiru_2026 = round(weighted.mean(as.numeric(both$jobs_60), both$population)),
    ipea_2019 = round(weighted.mean(as.numeric(both$CMATT60), both$population)),
    correlation = round(cor(both$jobs_60, both$CMATT60), 3))
})
compare
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("access-aop-capitals.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Three panels, one per city, each with two lines of the mean number of formal jobs reached within 60 minutes by public transport by income decile: computed here with the feeds of 2026, and published by Ipea for 2019."')
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
cmp <- function(k, what) compare[what, k]
ratio <- function(k) sprintf("%.2f", cmp(k, "peabiru_2026") / cmp(k, "ipea_2019"))
```

Cell by cell the two agree on who has more and who has less: the
correlations are `r sprintf("%.2f", cmp("spo", "correlation"))` in São
Paulo, `r sprintf("%.2f", cmp("rio", "correlation"))` in Rio de Janeiro and
`r sprintf("%.2f", cmp("bho", "correlation"))` in Belo Horizonte. The levels
differ, and not in one direction: for the average resident, the jobs within
an hour computed here are `r ratio("spo")` times Ipea's in São Paulo,
`r ratio("rio")` times in Rio de Janeiro (although this network has no
rail) and `r ratio("bho")` times in Belo Horizonte. Ipea's settings are
not the reason: in
Belo Horizonte, Ipea's window (06:00 to 08:00) and a limit of 30 or of 15
minutes on each walk move the mean by less than 2%.

```{r aop-variants, cache = slow}
bho <- st_drop_geometry(grids$bho)
variant <- function(hour, window, ...) {
  a <- accessibility(networks$bho, bho[bho$population > 0, ], bho[bho$jobs > 0, ],
                     opportunities_colnames = "jobs", mode = c("WALK", "TRANSIT"),
                     departure_datetime = as.POSIXct(sprintf("2026-11-17 %02d:00:00", hour),
                                                     tz = "America/Sao_Paulo"),
                     time_window = window, cutoffs = 60, max_trip_duration = 60,
                     n_threads = threads, progress = FALSE, ...)
  round(weighted.mean(as.numeric(a$accessibility), bho$population[match(a$id, bho$id)]))
}
c(as_above = variant(7, 60), window_06_08 = variant(6, 120),
  walk_30 = variant(6, 120, max_walk_time = 30), walk_15 = variant(6, 120, max_walk_time = 15))
```

The reason is in the timetables. The runs below were made apart from this
page, with Ipea's settings as its published code has them (departures 06:00
to 08:00, at most 30 minutes on each walk, trips of up to three hours with
the 60-minute limit applied to the median, relief from SRTM), and Ipea's
feeds of 2019 are not public, so the speeds are fitted, not read from them.

- **Belo Horizonte.** The feed of 2026 schedules every trip, at every hour,
  at 21 km/h (20.8 to 21.3 for nine trips in ten): a generated timetable,
  with no congestion in it. The city's own monitoring measured about 14
  km/h at the afternoon peak of 2019. With the same feed slowed to 16 km/h,
  the relief and Ipea's settings, the mean is 307,556 jobs against Ipea's
  308,011, the correlation rises from 0.91 to 0.97, and the two agree
  within 5% at every distance from the centre (as published here, the
  ratio went from 1.1 in the centre to 2.1 between 7.5 and 10 km). Of the
  1.37, the settings account for +1%, the relief for -3% and the speed for
  the rest.
- **São Paulo.** The feed changed the other way. On the 88 bus trips it
  shares with a sample of SPTrans's feed of about 2019, run times are 12%
  longer and morning headways 25% longer. Undoing both on every bus line
  (on a sample of 700 origins) brings the ratio from 0.75 to 0.89; the rest
  we did not explain (Ipea's network also had the intermunicipal buses of
  EMTU, and 88 trips are a small sample).
- **Rio de Janeiro.** There was no feed of 2019 to compare. This one
  schedules buses at about 22 km/h at 7 am, faster than at midday, while
  Ipea's own numbers for 2019 are lower at the peak than off it. Its level
  is reached (ratio 0.96, on a sample of 800 origins) when every trip takes
  one and a half times as long, about 15 km/h; and Ipea's network had the
  metro and the trains, which this one lacks. This is the least certain of
  the three.

Access computed from a timetable is only as good as the speeds in it: one
that ignores congestion overstated Belo Horizonte's by a third. The
differences between the two years on this page should not be read as
changes in the service.

## Time against fare

A fare is a limit of its own: the fastest trip is of little use to someone
who cannot pay for its transfers. peabiru routes with fares: a
*fare structure* says what each ride costs and which transfers are
discounted, `max_fare` keeps the fastest trip within a budget, and
`pareto_frontier()` gives the travel time at each budget.

**Why not in the three cities above.** With fares, public transport is
routed by the multi-criteria router, which does not handle lines given by
frequency (`detailed_itineraries()` refuses them, and with a fare
structure the travel times fall back to walking):
that excludes São Paulo and Rio de Janeiro, whose feeds do carry the fares
(`fare_attributes.txt`: R$ 5.30 the bus and R$ 5.40 the metro and the train
in São Paulo, R$ 9.38 the two together; in Rio de Janeiro R$ 5.00 on the
buses, with one transfer within three hours, and on the BRT, and R$ 15.00
to 24.85 on the executive lines). Belo Horizonte's feed has timetables and
fares by line (R$ 6.25 or 6.00 on all but two lines, which cost R$ 5.00 and
3.00), but its
[rules](https://prefeitura.pbh.gov.br/sumob/onibus/tipos-de-linha-e-servicos)
(page updated on 2026-09-04) cannot be written faithfully as a fare
structure:
a second ride within 90 minutes costs half the cheaper fare with the BHBUS
card, a transfer inside a MOVE station costs nothing, and the supplementary
lines have no integration; a structure without the free transfers at
stations would overstate the cost of the trunk-and-feeder trips the network
is built around. So this part uses **Porto Alegre**: the sample data of
the r5r project, version 2.5.0 (the centre and the neighbourhoods around
it, the buses of EPTC and the trains of Trensurb as of May 2019) and the
fare structure that comes with it. The feeds only have service in the afternoon, so the departures are
from 14:00 to 14:59 of Monday 13 May 2019.

```{r poa}
poa_url <- "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ipea/r5r/v2.5.0/r-package/inst/extdata/poa/"
poa_dir <- file.path(data_dir, "poa", "network")
dir.create(poa_dir, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
poa_file <- function(file, dir = dirname(poa_dir)) {
  dest <- file.path(dir, basename(file))
  if (!file.exists(dest)) download.file(paste0(poa_url, file), dest, mode = "wb", quiet = TRUE)
  dest
}
for (f in c("poa_osm.pbf", "poa_eptc.zip", "poa_trensurb.zip")) poa_file(f, poa_dir)
poa <- build_network(poa_dir)
fares <- read_fare_structure(poa_file("fares/fares_poa.zip"))
fares$fares_per_type
fares$fares_per_transfer
c(fares$max_discounted_transfers, fares$transfer_time_allowance)
```

A bus ride costs R$ 4.80 and a train ride R$ 4.50; two buses within 60
minutes cost R$ 7.20 together, a bus and a train R$ 8.37; one discounted
transfer per trip. For a city of your own, `setup_fare_structure(network,
base_fare, by = "MODE")` starts a structure from the feed, to be edited
into the city's rules (see [Fares](fares.html)).

The cells are the sample's grid (the same H3 cells as Ipea's; 1,227 of the
5,781 of the municipality), with the income quintile of each cell in the
whole city, from the same release as above (`R002`).

```{r poa-cells}
poa_cells <- read.csv(poa_file("poa_hexgrid.csv"))
poa_income <- read.csv(aop("population_2010_poa.csv"))[, c("id_hex", "R002")]
poa_cells <- merge(poa_cells, poa_income, by.x = "id", by.y = "id_hex", all.x = TRUE)
names(poa_cells)[names(poa_cells) == "R002"] <- "quintile"
poa_cells$jobs[is.na(poa_cells$jobs)] <- 0   # five cells of the sample have no count
poa_origins <- poa_cells[poa_cells$population > 0, ]
poa_destinations <- poa_cells[poa_cells$jobs > 0, ]
table(quintile = poa_origins$quintile, useNA = "ifany")
```

The sample covers the centre of the city, where incomes are higher: the
two poorest fifths of Porto Alegre have fewer of its cells than the others.

### What a budget leaves

The jobs within an hour with at most one bus fare (R$ 4.80: a single ride),
at most two (R$ 9.60: enough for either discounted transfer), and at any
fare:

```{r budget, cache = slow}
poa_departure <- as.POSIXct("2019-05-13 14:00:00", tz = "America/Sao_Paulo")
with_budget <- function(max_fare) {
  a <- accessibility(poa, poa_origins, poa_destinations, opportunities_colnames = "jobs",
                     mode = c("WALK", "TRANSIT"), departure_datetime = poa_departure,
                     time_window = 60, percentiles = 50, cutoffs = 60, max_trip_duration = 60,
                     fare_structure = fares, max_fare = max_fare, draws_per_minute = 1,
                     n_threads = threads, progress = FALSE)
  as.numeric(a$accessibility[match(poa_origins$id, a$id)])   # integers would overflow in the means
}
budget <- data.frame(poa_origins[, c("id", "population", "quintile")],
                     one = with_budget(4.8), two = with_budget(9.6), any = with_budget(Inf))
```

```{r budget-table}
kept <- function(rows) {
  m <- sapply(c("one", "two", "any"), function(b) weighted.mean(rows[[b]], rows$population))
  c(jobs_any_fare = round(m[["any"]]), kept_one_fare = round(100 * m[["one"]] / m[["any"]], 1),
    kept_two_fares = round(100 * m[["two"]] / m[["any"]], 1))
}
rbind(t(sapply(split(budget, budget$quintile), kept)), all = kept(budget))
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
k_all <- kept(budget)
k_q <- t(sapply(split(budget, budget$quintile), kept))
```

One bus fare, which pays a single ride, keeps
`r sprintf("%.0f%%", k_all[["kept_one_fare"]])` of the jobs that the average
resident of the sample reaches at any fare, and two fares
`r sprintf("%.0f%%", k_all[["kept_two_fares"]])`. The loss is larger where
incomes are lower: in the poorest fifth one fare keeps
`r sprintf("%.0f%%", k_q["1", "kept_one_fare"])`, in the richest
`r sprintf("%.0f%%", k_q["5", "kept_one_fare"])`. The sample is the
centre of the city only, so read the gradient rather than the levels.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("fare-budget-poa.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Grouped bars, one group per income quintile, of the mean share of jobs reached within 60 minutes by public transport in the Porto Alegre sample at any fare, with at most two bus fares, and with at most one."')
```

With fares the router samples departures in the window
(`draws_per_minute = 1`: one per minute) instead of trying every minute.

### The frontier of a trip

`pareto_frontier()` gives, for a pair, the travel time at each fare cutoff
where it changes: here the median over the departures of the hour, for
three trips (the names of the neighbourhoods are OpenStreetMap's).

```{r pareto}
pairs <- data.frame(
  from = c("89a9012aed3ffff", "89a9012aa7bffff", "89a9012830bffff"),
  to = c("89a9012aa7bffff", "89a9012992bffff", "89a901284c7ffff"),
  trip = c("Cavalhada to Praia de Belas", "Praia de Belas to Navegantes",
           "Petrópolis to Teresópolis"))
frontier <- do.call(rbind, lapply(1:3, function(i) {
  pf <- pareto_frontier(poa, poa_cells[poa_cells$id == pairs$from[i], ],
                        poa_cells[poa_cells$id == pairs$to[i], ],
                        departure_datetime = poa_departure, time_window = 60,
                        fare_structure = fares, fare_cutoffs = c(0, 4.5, 4.8, 7.2, 8.37, 9.6, 12),
                        seed = 42, progress = FALSE)
  data.frame(trip = pairs$trip[i], fare = round(pf$monetary_cost, 2), minutes = pf$travel_time)
}))
frontier
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
at <- function(i, fare) frontier$minutes[frontier$trip == pairs$trip[i] & abs(frontier$fare - fare) < 0.01]
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("pareto-poa.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Three step charts of the median travel time of the fastest trip within a fare budget, for three pairs of places in Porto Alegre: along a corridor one bus fare is enough; across town the discounted transfer of two buses halves the time; from Petrópolis to Teresópolis each step still buys time."')
```

Along a corridor, one fare is all it takes: `r at(1, 4.8)` minutes against
`r at(1, 0)` on foot, and a second ride saves a minute. Across town, one
fare buys a trip of `r at(2, 4.8)` minutes, most of it walking to a line
that goes there directly, and the R$ 7.20 of the discounted transfer cuts
it to `r at(2, 7.2)`. A budget of R$ 0 is the walk, when it fits in the two
hours of `max_trip_duration`; R$ 4.50 pays only the train.

### The trips themselves

`trip_options()`, peabiru's own planner, lists the trips worth considering
between two points: those that no other trip beats on time, fare, transfers
and walking at once.

From Jardim Carvalho, in the east, to Praia de Belas, leaving at 14:19:

```{r options}
res <- trip_options(poa, poa_cells[poa_cells$id == "89a9012917bffff", ],
                    poa_cells[poa_cells$id == "89a9012aa7bffff", ],
                    departure_datetime = as.POSIXct("2019-05-13 14:19:00", tz = "America/Sao_Paulo"),
                    time_window = 1, criteria = c("TIME", "FARE", "TRANSFERS"),
                    fare_structure = fares, drop_geometry = TRUE, progress = FALSE)
as.data.frame(res$options[, c("option", "total_time", "fare", "transfers", "walk_time", "description")])
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("trip-options-poa.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "A map of Porto Alegre with three trips from Jardim Carvalho to Praia de Belas, and beside it their time, fare and transfers: the fastest on three buses, one with two buses and the transfer discount, and the cheapest on a single bus with a long walk."')
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
op <- as.data.frame(res$options)
```

Three buses take `r round(op$total_time[1])` minutes and cost R$
`r sprintf("%.2f", op$fare[1])`; two, with the transfer discount,
`r round(op$total_time[2])` minutes for R$ `r sprintf("%.2f", op$fare[2])`;
a single bus costs R$ `r sprintf("%.2f", op$fare[3])` and takes
`r round(op$total_time[3])` minutes, `r round(op$walk_time[3])` of them on
foot. `res$itineraries` has the legs of each option, as an `sf` of lines
unless `drop_geometry = TRUE`.

One thing to know when reading the two side by side: `trip_options()` and
`detailed_itineraries()` do not search the walk to the first stop in the
same way as `pareto_frontier()` and the matrices do. For the trip across town above, the one-fare trip of the
frontier starts with a walk of 70 minutes to a direct line; neither
`detailed_itineraries()` nor `trip_options()` lists it (for the 14:05
departure, `travel_time_matrix()` gives 89 minutes with `max_rides = 1`,
and `detailed_itineraries()` no trip).

## What is measured, and what is not

- **Planned service, one day, one hour.** Departures from 07:00 to 07:59 on
  Tuesday 17 November 2026, from timetables and headways as published; no
  delays, no crowding, no congestion beyond what the timetables assume.
- **The feeds' own limits.** Rio de Janeiro: buses and BRT only. Belo
  Horizonte: buses only. São Paulo: SPTrans buses, Metrô and CPTM; no EMTU.
  Trips cannot leave the area cut around each city, and destinations are
  those of the municipality.
- **Old people, old jobs, new buses.** Residents and income from the Censo
  2010, opportunities from 2019, public transport from 2026.
- **Cells, not addresses.** Every trip starts and ends at the centre of an
  H3 cell (about 350 m apart), linked to the nearest street.
- **Fares.** Porto Alegre's are the sample structure for the 2019 sample
  network, not checked here against the tariff decrees of that year, on a
  part of the city and an afternoon hour.

## Variations

- **Another departure window or percentile.** `percentiles = c(25, 50, 75)`
  shows how much the access depends on the minute one leaves.
- **Gradual access.** `decay_function = "logistic"` instead of a hard limit.
- **Your own fares.** `setup_fare_structure()`, edited, then
  `write_fare_structure()` to keep or share it.
- **Large matrices on disk.** `output_dir` writes one CSV file per origin.

